,"academicOrientation":{"score":32,"confidence":0.9},"profileConfidence":0.94,"lowerConfidenceCandidates":{"centralFigures":[{"name":"Lydia Delectorskaya","confidence":0.68},{"name":"Olga Meerson","confidence":0.48}]},"model":"gpt-5.6-luna","promptVersion":8,"inputHash":"8edf9a91b8c0e033c98538941cb04797139cbfdb686ba21983f0cca392d7ac5b","reviewStatus":"unreviewed","validationWarnings":[]},"primarySubject":"Biography","secondarySubjects":["Arts & Criticism"],"confidence":"high","rationale":"The book is primarily a biography of Henri Matisse, detailed through his life and artistic contributions, with a strong emphasis on art and cultural context.","method":"llm","model":"gpt-4o-mini","reviewStatus":"generated","inputHash":"c91c0630ab4cb1c87226f696d88358d8600baae996e75a6774758aa4efa281c7","summary":"“If my story were ever to be written down truthfully from start to finish, it would amaze everyone,” wrote Henri Matisse. It is hard to believe today that Matisse, whose exhibitions draw huge crowds worldwide, was once almost universally reviled and ridiculed. His response was neither to protest nor to retreat; he simply pushed on from one innovation to the next, and left the world to draw its own conclusions. Unfortunately, these were generally false and often damaging. Throughout his life and afterward people fantasized about his models and circulated baseless fabrications about his private life. Fifty years after his death, Matisse the Master (the second half of the biography that began with the acclaimed The Unknown Matisse) shows us the painter as he saw himself. With unprecedented and unrestricted access to his voluminous family correspondence, and other new material in private archives, Hilary Spurling documents a lifetime of desperation and self-doubt exacerbated by Matisse’s attempts to counteract the violence and disruption of the twentieth century in paintings that now seem effortlessly serene, radiant, and stable. Here for the first time is the truth about Matisse’s models, especially two Russians: his pupil Olga Meerson and the extraordinary Lydia Delectorskaya, who became his studio manager, secretary, and companion in the last two decades of his life.","displaySummary":"With unprecedented and unrestricted access to his voluminous family correspondence, and other new material in private archives, Hilary Spurling documents a lifetime of desperation and self-doubt exacerbated by Matisse’s attempts to counteract the violence and disruption of the twentieth century in paintings that now seem effortlessly serene, radiant, and stable. Here for the first time is the truth about Matisse’s models, especially two Russians: his pupil Olga Meerson and the extraordinary Lydia Delectorskaya, who became his studio manager, secretary, and companion in the last two decades of his life.","primaryTopic":"Artistic Biography","subjectEvidence":{"primarySubject":"Biography","confidence":"high","method":"evidence_score","candidates":[{"subject":"Biography","score":8,"evidenceCount":1}],"evidence":[{"source":"llm_classifier","rawLabel":"Biography","mappedSubject":"Biography","score":8,"confidence":"high","note":"The book is primarily a biography of Henri Matisse, detailed through his life and artistic contributions, with a strong emphasis on art and cultural context.","id":"subject-evidence-book-matisse-the-master-a-life-of-henri-matisse-the-conquest-of-colour-1909-1954-hilary-spurling-1"},{"source":"google_books","scheme":"google_books_category","rawLabel":"Biography & Autobiography","mappedSubject":"Memoir & Autobiography","score":7,"confidence":"high","sourceId":"source-google-books-summary-matisse-the-master-a-life-of-henri-matisse-the-conquest-of-colour-1909-1954-hilary-spurling","id":"subject-evidence-book-matisse-the-master-a-life-of-henri-matisse-the-conquest-of-colour-1909-1954-hilary-spurling-5"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","rawLabel":"Matisse, henri, 1869-1954","mappedSubject":"Biography","score":6,"confid